  </div>

</div></div></section>
<section class="nb-cell nb-cell--markdown"><div class="nb-markdown"><h2><strong>Why This Supplement Exists</strong></h2>
<p>In Module 3, we checked the LINE assumptions on a model that was already well-behaved (<code>mpg ~ wt</code>). Every plot looked clean, so it was hard to see what a <em>violation</em> actually looks like.</p>
<p>This supplement is a <strong>reference sheet</strong>. For each letter in <strong>LINE</strong>, it shows:</p>
<ul>
<li>Why the simplest possible check (a plain scatterplot) is not enough, and what we look at instead.</li>
<li>Side-by-side visuals of what a <strong>violation</strong> looks like, and why.</li>
<li>What the plot should look like when the assumption is <strong>satisfied</strong>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Nothing here replaces Module 3. Use this as a gallery to compare your own residual plots against when you are not sure what you are looking at.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="nb-cell nb-cell--markdown"><div class="nb-markdown"><h2><strong>Setup</strong></h2>
</div></section>
<section class="nb-cell nb-cell--code"><div class="nb-input"><div class="nb-prompt nb-prompt--in">In [1]:</div><pre class="nb-code"><code class="hljs language-python"><span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> numpy <span class="hljs-keyword">as</span> np
<span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> pandas <span class="hljs-keyword">as</span> pd
<span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> matplotlib.pyplot <span class="hljs-keyword">as</span> plt
<span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> seaborn <span class="hljs-keyword">as</span> sns
<span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> scipy.stats <span class="hljs-keyword">as</span> stats
<span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> statsmodels.api <span class="hljs-keyword">as</span> sm

sns.set_theme(style=<span class="hljs-string">&quot;whitegrid&quot;</span>)

<span class="hljs-comment"># Shared color palette (matches Module 3)</span>
BLUE      = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;#438BC4&quot;</span>   <span class="hljs-comment"># &quot;good&quot; / reference examples</span>
LIGHTBLUE = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;#8CC1E9&quot;</span>   <span class="hljs-comment"># secondary accent</span>
SALMON    = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;#E8998D&quot;</span>   <span class="hljs-comment"># &quot;problem&quot; / violation examples</span>
NAVY      = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;#12284B&quot;</span>
CRIMSON   = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;crimson&quot;</span>

np.random.seed(<span class="hljs-number">211</span>)</code></pre></div></section>
<section class="nb-cell nb-cell--markdown"><div class="nb-markdown"><h2><strong>1. Linearity</strong></h2>
</div></section>
<section class="nb-cell nb-cell--markdown"><div class="nb-markdown"><h3><strong>1.1 Why a Plain Scatterplot Is Not Enough</strong></h3>
<p>The most natural instinct is to eyeball a scatterplot of Y against X and ask "does this look like a line?" That check fails more often than it should, for a simple reason:</p>
<p><strong>A strong linear trend hides curvature.</strong> When the overall relationship is strong (large range on both axes, a clear upward or downward drift), any leftover curve gets visually swallowed by the trend itself. Your eye locks onto the slope and never notices the bend.</p>
<p>A <strong>residual plot</strong> fixes this by removing the trend on purpose. Once the straight-line trend is subtracted out, the only thing left on the page is what the line failed to explain. If a curve is hiding in the data, subtracting the line does not remove it, it isolates it. Any leftover pattern is now the <em>entire</em> picture, not a small deduction from a big trend, so it becomes easy to spot.</p>
<p>The cell below fits a straight line to a dataset that has a real quadratic curve baked in, and shows both views side by side.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="nb-cell nb-cell--code"><div class="nb-input"><div class="nb-prompt nb-prompt--in">In [2]:</div><pre class="nb-code"><code class="hljs language-python"><span class="hljs-comment"># A dataset with a genuine curve: Y bends downward as X grows</span>
x_curve = np.linspace(<span class="hljs-number">1</span>, <span class="hljs-number">10</span>, <span class="hljs-number">80</span>)
y_curve = <span class="hljs-number">2</span> + <span class="hljs-number">3</span>*x_curve - <span class="hljs-number">0.25</span>*x_curve**<span class="hljs-number">2</span> + np.random.normal(<span class="hljs-number">0</span>, <span class="hljs-number">1.2</span>, <span class="hljs-number">80</span>)

<span class="hljs-comment"># Fit a straight line anyway (ignoring the curve)</span>
X_design = sm.add_constant(x_curve)
curve_model = sm.OLS(y_curve, X_design).fit()
curve_fitted = curve_model.fittedvalues
curve_resid  = curve_model.resid

fig, axes = plt.subplots(<span class="hljs-number">1</span>, <span class="hljs-number">2</span>, figsize=(<span class="hljs-number">13</span>, <span class="hljs-number">5</span>))

<span class="hljs-comment"># --- Plain scatterplot with the fitted line ---</span>
axes[<span class="hljs-number">0</span>].scatter(x_curve, y_curve, color=BLUE, edgecolor=<span class="hljs-string">&quot;white&quot;</span>, s=<span class="hljs-number">55</span>)
axes[<span class="hljs-number">0</span>].plot(x_curve, curve_fitted, color=CRIMSON, linewidth=<span class="hljs-number">2</span>, label=<span class="hljs-string">&quot;Fitted straight line&quot;</span>)
axes[<span class="hljs-number">0</span>].set_title(<span class="hljs-string">&quot;Plain Scatterplot\n(the curve is easy to miss)&quot;</span>)
axes[<span class="hljs-number">0</span>].set_xlabel(<span class="hljs-string">&quot;X&quot;</span>)
axes[<span class="hljs-number">0</span>].set_ylabel(<span class="hljs-string">&quot;Y&quot;</span>)
axes[<span class="hljs-number">0</span>].legend(loc=<span class="hljs-string">&quot;lower right&quot;</span>, fontsize=<span class="hljs-number">8</span>)
axes[<span class="hljs-number">0</span>].annotate(<span class="hljs-string">&quot;Trend hides the bend:\nstill looks roughly linear&quot;</span>,
                  xy=(x_curve[<span class="hljs-number">15</span>], y_curve[<span class="hljs-number">15</span>]), xytext=(<span class="hljs-number">0.5</span>, <span class="hljs-number">0.02</span>),
                  textcoords=<span class="hljs-string">&quot;axes fraction&quot;</span>, ha=<span class="hljs-string">&quot;center&quot;</span>, fontsize=<span class="hljs-number">9</span>,
                  color=NAVY, bbox=<span class="hljs-built_in">dict</span>(boxstyle=<span class="hljs-string">&quot;round,pad=0.35&quot;</span>, fc=<span class="hljs-string">&quot;white&quot;</span>, ec=BLUE, alpha=<span class="hljs-number">0.9</span>),
                  arrowprops=<span class="hljs-built_in">dict</span>(arrowstyle=<span class="hljs-string">&quot;-&gt;&quot;</span>, color=BLUE, lw=<span class="hljs-number">1.5</span>))

<span class="hljs-comment"># --- Residual plot for the same model ---</span>
axes[<span class="hljs-number">1</span>].scatter(curve_fitted, curve_resid, color=SALMON, edgecolor=<span class="hljs-string">&quot;white&quot;</span>, s=<span class="hljs-number">55</span>)
axes[<span class="hljs-number">1</span>].axhline(<span class="hljs-number">0</span>, color=CRIMSON, linestyle=<span class="hljs-string">&quot;--&quot;</span>, linewidth=<span class="hljs-number">2</span>)
axes[<span class="hljs-number">1</span>].set_title(<span class="hljs-string">&quot;Residual Plot for the Same Model\n(the curve is now obvious)&quot;</span>)
axes[<span class="hljs-number">1</span>].set_xlabel(<span class="hljs-string">&quot;Fitted Values&quot;</span>)
axes[<span class="hljs-number">1</span>].set_ylabel(<span class="hljs-string">&quot;Residuals&quot;</span>)
peak_i = np.argmax(curve_resid)
axes[<span class="hljs-number">1</span>].annotate(<span class="hljs-string">&quot;Curve revealed:\nresiduals arc away from zero&quot;</span>,
                  xy=(curve_fitted[peak_i], curve_resid[peak_i]), xytext=(<span class="hljs-number">0.5</span>, <span class="hljs-number">0.05</span>),
                  textcoords=<span class="hljs-string">&quot;axes fraction&quot;</span>, ha=<span class="hljs-string">&quot;center&quot;</span>, fontsize=<span class="hljs-number">9</span>,
                  color=NAVY, bbox=<span class="hljs-built_in">dict</span>(boxstyle=<span class="hljs-string">&quot;round,pad=0.35&quot;</span>, fc=<span class="hljs-string">&quot;white&quot;</span>, ec=SALMON, alpha=<span class="hljs-number">0.9</span>),
                  arrowprops=<span class="hljs-built_in">dict</span>(arrowstyle=<span class="hljs-string">&quot;-&gt;&quot;</span>, color=SALMON, lw=<span class="hljs-number">1.5</span>))

plt.tight_layout()
plt.show()</code></pre></div><div class="nb-outputs"><div class="nb-output nb-output--image"><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt=""></div></div></section>
<section class="nb-cell nb-cell--markdown"><div class="nb-markdown"><p><strong>How to read this:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Left:</strong> the raw scatterplot still "looks" roughly linear at a glance. The overall upward-then-downward drift is subtle enough to explain away as noise.</li>
<li><strong>Right:</strong> the exact same model's residuals form a clean arc. Once the straight-line trend is removed, the leftover U-shape (really an upside-down U here) is unmistakable.</li>
</ul>
<p>This is why we never judge Linearity from the raw scatterplot alone. <strong>The residual plot is the correct tool because it strips away the trend and leaves only the model's mistakes, which is exactly what a curve shows up in.</strong></p>
</div></section>
<section class="nb-cell nb-cell--markdown"><div class="nb-markdown"><h3><strong>1.2 What a Linearity Violation Looks Like</strong></h3>
<p>Below are three residual-vs-fitted patterns. The first is what we want to see; the other two are common violations, each with a different shape and a different real-world cause.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="nb-cell nb-cell--code nb-cell--long"><div class="nb-input"><div class="nb-prompt nb-prompt--in">In [3]:</div><pre class="nb-code"><code class="hljs language-python">np.random.seed(<span class="hljs-number">1</span>)
fitted_grid = np.linspace(<span class="hljs-number">0</span>, <span class="hljs-number">10</span>, <span class="hljs-number">90</span>)

<span class="hljs-comment"># Good: random scatter, no pattern</span>
good_resid = np.random.normal(<span class="hljs-number">0</span>, <span class="hljs-number">1</span>, <span class="hljs-number">90</span>)

<span class="hljs-comment"># Violation 1: U-shape (model misses curvature that bends upward at the edges)</span>
u_resid = <span class="hljs-number">0.35</span>*(fitted_grid - <span class="hljs-number">5</span>)**<span class="hljs-number">2</span> - <span class="hljs-number">3</span> + np.random.normal(<span class="hljs-number">0</span>, <span class="hljs-number">0.9</span>, <span class="hljs-number">90</span>)

<span class="hljs-comment"># Violation 2: wave / cyclical pattern (model misses a repeating pattern, e.g. seasonality)</span>
wave_resid = <span class="hljs-number">2.5</span>*np.sin(fitted_grid * <span class="hljs-number">1.1</span>) + np.random.normal(<span class="hljs-number">0</span>, <span class="hljs-number">0.6</span>, <span class="hljs-number">90</span>)

fig, axes = plt.subplots(<span class="hljs-number">1</span>, <span class="hljs-number">3</span>, figsize=(<span class="hljs-number">16</span>, <span class="hljs-number">4.8</span>))
data = [good_resid, u_resid, wave_resid]
titles = [
    <span class="hljs-string">&quot;Satisfies Linearity\n(random scatter, no shape)&quot;</span>,
    <span class="hljs-string">&quot;Violation: U-Shape\n(model misses curvature)&quot;</span>,
    <span class="hljs-string">&quot;Violation: Wave Pattern\n(model misses a repeating trend)&quot;</span>
]
colors = [BLUE, SALMON, SALMON]

<span class="hljs-keyword">for</span> ax, resid, title, color <span class="hljs-keyword">in</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">zip</span>(axes, data, titles, colors):
    ax.scatter(fitted_grid, resid, color=color, edgecolor=<span class="hljs-string">&quot;white&quot;</span>, s=<span class="hljs-number">45</span>)
    ax.axhline(<span class="hljs-number">0</span>, color=CRIMSON, linestyle=<span class="hljs-string">&quot;--&quot;</span>, linewidth=<span class="hljs-number">2</span>)
    ax.set_title(title)
    ax.set_xlabel(<span class="hljs-string">&quot;Fitted Values&quot;</span>)
    ax.set_ylabel(<span class="hljs-string">&quot;Residuals&quot;</span>)

<span class="hljs-comment"># --- Annotations ---</span>
axes[<span class="hljs-number">0</span>].annotate(<span class="hljs-string">&quot;No shape:\npoints scatter randomly\nabove and below zero&quot;</span>,
                  xy=(<span class="hljs-number">5</span>, <span class="hljs-number">0.1</span>), xytext=(<span class="hljs-number">0.5</span>, <span class="hljs-number">0.06</span>), textcoords=<span class="hljs-string">&quot;axes fraction&quot;</span>, ha=<span class="hljs-string">&quot;center&quot;</span>,
                  fontsize=<span class="hljs-number">8.5</span>, color=NAVY, bbox=<span class="hljs-built_in">dict</span>(boxstyle=<span class="hljs-string">&quot;round,pad=0.3&quot;</span>, fc=<span class="hljs-string">&quot;white&quot;</span>, ec=BLUE, alpha=<span class="hljs-number">0.9</span>))

