<section class="nb-cell nb-cell--markdown"><div class="nb-markdown"><p><strong>How to read this:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Left (satisfied):</strong> points are scattered evenly above and below the zero line across the whole range, with no visible shape. This is the target.</li>
<li><strong>Middle (U-shape):</strong> residuals are negative in the middle and positive at both ends (or the reverse). This means the true relationship curves, and a straight line under-predicts in some regions and over-predicts in others in a systematic way. Fix: add a polynomial term, transform X or Y, or use a nonlinear model.</li>
<li><strong>Right (wave pattern):</strong> residuals rise and fall in a repeating rhythm. This usually means a cyclical or periodic effect (e.g. seasonality, time-of-day effects) is not captured by the model. Fix: add a term that captures the cycle, or use a model built for periodic data.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>What "satisfied" should look like:</strong> a shapeless cloud of points, centered on zero, with no curve, no wave, and no visible structure of any kind, no matter where you look along the x-axis.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="nb-cell nb-cell--markdown"><div class="nb-markdown"><h2><strong>2. Independence</strong></h2>
</div></section>
<section class="nb-cell nb-cell--markdown"><div class="nb-markdown"><p><strong>What it means:</strong> each residual should carry no information about the next one. Knowing that observation #5 had a large positive error should tell you nothing about whether observation #6 will also have a large positive error.</p>
<p><strong>Why this matters:</strong> independence is usually at risk when observations have a natural order, most commonly <strong>time</strong> (daily sales, monthly readings, repeated measurements on the same subject) or <strong>grouping</strong> (students within the same classroom, patients within the same hospital). When residuals are not independent, standard errors from the model become unreliable, this makes p-values look more confident than they should be, even though the coefficient estimates themselves may still be fine.</p>
<p><strong>How to check it:</strong> plot residuals against their <strong>order of collection</strong> (or against time, if the data is a time series). If independence holds, the residuals should bounce around zero randomly with no drift, no streaks, and no rhythm from one point to the next.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="nb-cell nb-cell--code nb-cell--long"><div class="nb-input"><div class="nb-prompt nb-prompt--in">In [4]:</div><pre class="nb-code"><code class="hljs language-python">np.random.seed(<span class="hljs-number">7</span>)
