<section class="nb-cell nb-cell--markdown"><div class="nb-markdown"><p><strong>How to read this:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Left (satisfied):</strong> the line zig-zags around zero with no memory of where it just was. Durbin-Watson lands close to <strong>2</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>Middle (positive autocorrelation):</strong> once a residual is positive, the next several tend to stay positive too, and the same for negative runs. The line drifts in long "waves" instead of bouncing randomly. This is the classic symptom of time-ordered data (e.g. daily measurements where today's leftover error resembles yesterday's). Durbin-Watson lands well <strong>below 2</strong>, closer to 0.</li>
<li><strong>Right (cyclical pattern):</strong> residuals repeat a rhythm, a sign that some periodic factor (season, day-of-week, business cycle) is influencing the outcome but is not in the model. Durbin-Watson can look unusual in either direction depending on the cycle length, which is exactly why we always look at the plot itself and not just the number.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>What "satisfied" should look like:</strong> a residual-vs-order plot with no visible streaks, drift, or rhythm, points scattered randomly above and below zero regardless of which observation came before, paired with a Durbin-Watson statistic near 2.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="nb-cell nb-cell--markdown"><div class="nb-markdown"><h2><strong>3. Normality</strong></h2>
</div></section>
<section class="nb-cell nb-cell--markdown"><div class="nb-markdown"><p><strong>What it means:</strong> the residuals, as a group, should be shaped like a bell curve centered on zero. This assumption matters most for the trustworthiness of p-values and confidence intervals, not for the coefficient estimates themselves.</p>
<h3><strong>3.1 Which Check to Use, Based on Sample Size</strong></h3>
<p>Not every dataset should be checked the same way, and not every formal test behaves the same way either. Shapiro-Wilk is built for small samples but loses power once n grows. Jarque-Bera and Omnibus both rely on skewness and kurtosis combined into a χ² statistic — an approximation that needs a reasonably sized sample to hold up, so both are poor choices when n is small, but good ones in the middle range. Omnibus applies a small-sample correction to the skewness/kurtosis estimates before combining them, which makes it somewhat more reliable than Jarque-Bera at the lower end of that range; at larger n the two converge and give essentially the same result. And every p-value test — Shapiro-Wilk, Jarque-Bera, and Omnibus alike — becomes oversensitive once the sample gets very large: with enough data, the test will flag tiny, harmless deviations from perfect normality as "statistically significant," even when they change nothing about the model's usefulness. The table below is a decision framework, not a single fixed rule.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Sample Size</th>
<th>Recommended Strategy</th>
<th>Why This Approach Works</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>Small Samples (n &lt; 30)</strong></td>
<td>Q-Q Plot + Shapiro-Wilk Test</td>
<td>Shapiro-Wilk handles small-sample order statistics best. (Avoid Jarque-Bera and Omnibus here; both lack power in small samples, though Omnibus's correction makes it the less-bad of the two if you must use one).</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Medium-to-Large (30 ≤ n ≤ 500)</strong></td>
<td>Q-Q Plot + Jarque-Bera and/or Omnibus Test (or Skewness &amp; Kurtosis)</td>
<td>Both tests shine in this range (especially in finance/econometrics) because their χ² distribution assumption holds reliably without being overwhelmed by n. Statsmodels reports both together by default — if they agree, trust the result; if they diverge, it's usually a few outliers pulling Jarque-Bera's raw kurtosis term harder than Omnibus's corrected one, so treat it as a cue to inspect the Q-Q plot.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Very Large Samples (n &gt; 500)</strong></td>
<td>Q-Q Plot + Raw Skewness &amp; Kurtosis (Avoid all p-value tests)</td>
<td>Rely directly on skewness (target: −0.5 to 0.5) and excess kurtosis (target: −1 to 1). All hypothesis tests—including Jarque-Bera and Omnibus—become overly sensitive here.</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>The one constant across every row: the Q-Q plot never leaves the toolkit.</strong> It is the only check that lets you <em>see</em> what's wrong, a formal test or a skewness number can tell you something is off, but only the plot tells you whether it's one extreme outlier, a skewed tail, or a systematic heavy-tail problem.</p>
<h4><strong>What Each Test Actually Measures</strong></h4>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Test</th>
<th>What It Measures</th>
<th>Basis</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>Shapiro-Wilk</strong></td>
<td>Whether sorted residuals line up the way normally-distributed values should</td>
<td>Correlation with expected normal order statistics — no skewness/kurtosis involved</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Jarque-Bera</strong></td>
<td>Joint deviation of skewness and kurtosis from normal (0 and 3, respectively)</td>
<td>Raw sample skewness and kurtosis, plugged directly into a χ² formula</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Omnibus (D'Agostino-Pearson)</strong></td>
<td>Same joint deviation as Jarque-Bera</td>
<td>Sample skewness and kurtosis, each first transformed into a bias-corrected, near-normal statistic before being combined into χ²</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<h4><strong>Differences Between the Three</strong></h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Shapiro-Wilk vs. the other two:</strong> fundamentally different methods. Shapiro-Wilk is a shape-matching test on sorted values; Jarque-Bera and Omnibus are moment-based tests built entirely from skewness and kurtosis. They just happen to compete for use in overlapping sample-size ranges.</li>
<li><strong>Jarque-Bera vs. Omnibus:</strong> the same underlying method — both are skewness-and-kurtosis tests producing a χ² statistic with 2 degrees of freedom. The only difference is that Omnibus corrects the skewness/kurtosis estimates for small-sample bias before combining them, while Jarque-Bera uses the raw estimates. This makes Omnibus modestly more reliable at smaller n and slightly less prone to being skewed by a handful of outliers. At larger n, the correction becomes negligible and the two tests converge.</li>
</ul>
</div></section>
<section class="nb-cell nb-cell--markdown"><div class="nb-markdown"><h3><strong>3.2 What a Normality Violation Looks Like on a Q-Q Plot</strong></h3>
<p>A Q-Q plot compares your residuals' quantiles against the quantiles a perfect normal distribution would produce. <strong>If residuals are normal, the points fall on the diagonal reference line.</strong> Any consistent bend away from that line is a specific, readable signal.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="nb-cell nb-cell--code nb-cell--long"><div class="nb-input"><div class="nb-prompt nb-prompt--in">In [5]:</div><pre class="nb-code"><code class="hljs language-python">np.random.seed(<span class="hljs-number">3</span>)
n = <span class="hljs-number">200</span>

normal_data      = np.random.normal(<span class="hljs-number">0</span>, <span class="hljs-number">1</span>, n)
right_skew_data  = np.random.exponential(<span class="hljs-number">1.2</span>, n) - <span class="hljs-number">1.2</span>      <span class="hljs-comment"># long right tail</span>
