<section class="nb-cell nb-cell--markdown"><div class="nb-markdown"><p><strong>How to read this:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Satisfied (far left):</strong> points track the diagonal line closely from end to end. No shape, no drift, just noise around the line.</li>
<li><strong>Right-skewed:</strong> points bend <strong>upward and away from the line at the top right</strong>. The largest residuals are more extreme than a normal distribution would predict, a long right tail.</li>
<li><strong>Left-skewed:</strong> the mirror image, points bend <strong>downward and away from the line at the bottom left</strong>. A long left tail.</li>
<li><strong>Heavy tails:</strong> points hug the line in the middle but curve sharply away at <strong>both ends</strong>, forming a shallow "S." Extreme values (both very high and very low) happen more often than a normal curve predicts. This is common with outliers or a distribution with a sharper peak and fatter tails.</li>
<li><strong>Light tails:</strong> the opposite "S", points curl <strong>inward</strong> at both ends instead of flying outward. Extreme values are rarer than normal would predict, often seen with bounded or uniform-like data.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>What "satisfied" should look like:</strong> points falling on (or very close to) the 45-degree reference line across its entire length, with no systematic bend at either end.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="nb-cell nb-cell--markdown"><div class="nb-markdown"><h2><strong>4. Equal Variance</strong></h2>
</div></section>
<section class="nb-cell nb-cell--markdown"><div class="nb-markdown"><p><strong>What it means:</strong> the spread (variance) of the residuals should stay roughly constant across every level of the fitted values. The model should be about equally "wrong" for small predictions and large predictions, not increasingly wrong as predictions grow.</p>
<p><strong>Why it matters:</strong> when variance changes systematically (<strong>heteroscedasticity</strong>), the model's standard errors, and therefore its p-values and confidence intervals, become unreliable, even though the coefficient estimates themselves may still be roughly correct.</p>
<p><strong>How to check it:</strong> the same residual-vs-fitted plot used for Linearity. This time, instead of looking for curves, look at the <strong>vertical spread</strong> of the dots as you scan left to right.</p>
</div></section>
