<section class="nb-cell nb-cell--markdown"><div class="nb-markdown"><p><strong>How to read this:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Satisfied (far left):</strong> the vertical "band" of points stays about the same width from left to right. The model is equally precise everywhere.</li>
<li><strong>Funnel-out:</strong> the band starts narrow on the left and widens on the right, the classic megaphone shape. The model's errors grow larger as predictions grow larger. Common when the outcome variable spans a wide range (e.g. income, sales), since large values naturally have more room to be off by a large amount.</li>
<li><strong>Funnel-in:</strong> the reverse, wide on the left, narrow on the right. Less common, but happens when a variable has a natural floor or ceiling that residuals compress against as fitted values rise.</li>
<li><strong>Bowtie:</strong> narrow at both ends, wide in the middle. This suggests the model performs best at extreme predicted values but has the most trouble distinguishing cases in the middle range, often a sign that an important predictor or interaction is missing.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>What "satisfied" should look like:</strong> a horizontal band of roughly constant vertical thickness across the entire range of fitted values, no widening, no narrowing, and no bulging at any point. A Breusch-Pagan p-value above 0.05 backs up what the plot shows.</p>
</div></section>
